    intro: "The sixth day buys you Ella, and Ella is the place most people remember. Five days forces you to ride the hill train and then immediately drive back down; six lets you get off, sleep there, and spend a morning walking. That single change turns the tea country from a scenic transit into a destination. The rest of the route is the same anticlockwise loop — north to the cultural triangle, across to Kandy, up into the hills, down to the coast — with the drives spread more kindly.",
    bestFor: "A first full trip to the island for people who want hills and hiking rather than beaches.",
    quickAnswer: "Six days lets you add Ella, which is the day most five-day itineraries have to cut. Day 1 Colombo, day 2 Sigiriya and Dambulla, day 3 Kandy, day 4 the tea country and the hill train to Ella, day 5 Ella itself — Little Adam's Peak, the Nine Arch bridge, waterfall abseiling — day 6 the drive south to Galle and back to Colombo. Six days covers culture, hills and one coastal day.",
