    logistics: [
      { heading: "Getting around", content: "Sri Lanka is small on the map and slow on the ground. Colombo to Kandy is 115 km and takes three to four hours; Kandy to Ella is 140 km and takes four to five. Distances mislead, so plan by hours, not kilometres. A car with a driver runs roughly USD 55-75 a day including fuel and the driver's accommodation, and for two or more people it costs about the same as separate transfers while saving hours. Intercity trains are cheap and scenic but slow and often full — the Kandy to Ella line is worth taking for the ride itself, not as transport." },
      { heading: "Money and cards", content: "The currency is the Sri Lankan rupee. Cards work in Colombo hotels, larger restaurants and supermarkets; almost nothing else. Carry cash for tuk-tuks, entrance fees at smaller sites, village lunches and tips. ATMs are reliable in Colombo, Kandy, Galle and Negombo and thin everywhere else, so draw before you leave a town." },
      { heading: "Visa", content: "Most nationalities need an ETA before arrival, applied for online at the official Sri Lankan immigration site. Apply a few days ahead rather than at the airport, and use the government site directly — the copycat sites that rank alongside it charge a markup for the same document." },
      { heading: "What to wear at temples", content: "Shoulders and knees covered at every Buddhist site, and shoes off before you enter — that includes Dambulla, the Temple of the Tooth in Kandy and Colombo's Gangaramaya. Socks are worth carrying: stone courtyards get genuinely painful underfoot by midday. Never pose with your back to a Buddha image for a photograph; it is treated as a serious offence and tourists have been deported for it." },
      { heading: "When to go", content: "Sri Lanka has two monsoons hitting opposite coasts, so there is no bad month for the whole island. December to March is dry on the west and south coasts and in the hill country, which is the season for Colombo, Galle, Mirissa and Ella. May to September is dry on the east coast. April and October are the inter-monsoon months and can be wet anywhere. Whale watching off Mirissa runs roughly November to April." },
      { heading: "Tipping", content: "Tipping is expected but small. Roughly 10% at restaurants where service is not already added, LKR 500-1000 a day for a driver-guide, and LKR 200-500 for a safari tracker. Rounding up a tuk-tuk fare is enough." },
      { heading: "The hill-country train", content: "The Kandy to Ella line is the one train worth planning around, and the best section is Nanu Oya to Ella — about four hours through tea estates and the Nine Arch bridge. Reserved seats open 30 days ahead and sell out within hours; unreserved second and third class are always available on the day and are standing-room by Nuwara Eliya. Sit on the right-hand side heading south for the valley views. If a reserved seat is not available, take the drive and use the train for the short Nanu Oya-Ella leg only." },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { question: "Is six days better than five in Sri Lanka?", answer: "Noticeably, yes. The sixth day is what lets you sleep in Ella instead of riding through it. Little Adam's Peak, the Nine Arch bridge and the waterfalls all need a morning you do not have on a five-day loop." },
      { question: "Ella or Nuwara Eliya — where should I sleep?", answer: "Ella, for most people. It is warmer, the walks start from the town, and the food is better. Nuwara Eliya is worth the drive through and a tea factory stop, but the town itself is quiet and cold." },
      { question: "How hard is Little Adam's Peak?", answer: "Easy. It is about 45 minutes up a stepped path, suitable for most fitness levels and children. Ella Rock is the harder one — three hours round trip, poorly signed, and worth taking a guide for." },
      { question: "Can I fit a safari into six days?", answer: "Only by cutting something. Udawalawe is two hours south of Ella and could replace the Galle day, which turns this into a hills-and-wildlife trip rather than a hills-and-coast one. Doing both properly is the seven-day itinerary." },
      { question: "Do I need to book the Ella train in advance?", answer: "For a reserved seat, yes — they open 30 days ahead and sell out in hours, especially December to March. Unreserved second and third class can be bought on the day and always have room, though you will likely stand from Nanu Oya." },
      { question: "What is the total driving time on this route?", answer: "Around 18 hours over six days, with the longest single stretch being the four hours from Colombo to Sigiriya on day two and the four hours from Ella to Galle on day six. The middle days are two to three hours each." },
    ],
